    "summary": "Stellar early‑'73 set with killer China>Rider and soaring The Other One",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a tight first set and a second‑set marathon highlighted by the classic China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue, an inspired The Other One → Eyes Of The World → Morning Dew stretch, and a fiery El Paso. Jerry’s vocals are on point, Phil lays down a powerful bass foundation, and Keith’s keyboards shine throughout.",
    "review": "On February 28, 1973 the Dead took the Salt Palace into a high‑energy early‑’73 groove that still feels fresh today. The first set rolls through familiar staples—Cold Rain And Snow, Beat It On Down The Line, Sugaree and a crisp El Paso—that showcase tight vocal harmonies and Phil’s “blow‑torching” bass on Jack Straw. The second set is where the magic truly erupts: a seamless China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider transition launches into an inspired sequence of The Other One → Eyes Of The World → Morning Dew, while Row Jimmy provides a soulful interlude before the band storms through Truckin' and a marathon jam that culminates in a soaring Sugar Magnolia. Jerry’s lead vocals are strong (especially on The Promised Land), his guitar work solid, and Keith Godchaux’s organ shines on El Paso, backed by Billy Kreutzmann’s dynamic drumming.",
